AB  - Automated generation of software test cases are one of the crucial tasks in modern software engineering, while the traditional method of manually writing test cases is time-consuming, prone to making errors and often fails to achieve contextual or linguistic accuracy. This paper proposes a Retrieval-Augmented Generation based method, which integrates dynamic knowledge retrieval with LLMs to generate highly faithful and contextually grounded test cases. Unlike general fine-tuned LLMs, which require extensive retraining, the proposed RAG pipeline uses lanceDB to store domain related knowledge and SentenceTransformers to semantically retrieve knowledge from the database, thus offering more reliability with much lower computational cost. Besides this, the suggested pipeline allows multilingual test case generation done by the LLM model across English and other Indian regional languages like Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, Malayalam and Kannada making software test more inclusive for language sensitive software ecosystems. The generated test cases are observed to be semantically aligned with the retrieved context and real-world user stories thus improving factual consistency and software reliability. Experimental evaluation shows consistent faithfulness scores, showing the effectiveness of the RAG pipeline and LLMs generation. Results confirm the embeddings quality, retrieval accuracy and reasoning capability within the model. Also, the architecture can be optionally extended for user feedback-based continual learning by integrating it with QA platforms such as Jira via Model Context Protocol (MCP) which gives the possibility to modify the model as per the project requirements. Overall, this study shows that RAG based models could provide scalable, multilingual and intelligent automation in future software quality assurance.
